WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.947
Book Two, Proposition Ten.

2
00:00:01.947 --> 00:00:19.921
If a segment be bisected, and a segment be added to it in a segment, the square on the whole with the added segment and the square on the added segment both together are double of the square on the half and of the square described on the segment made up of the half and the added segment as on one segment.

3
00:00:19.921 --> 00:00:25.090
Let segment A B be bisected at C, and let segment B D be added to it in a straight line.

4
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:35.248
From C, draw C E at right angles to A B by One Eleven, make it equal to A C or C B by One Three, and join E A and E B.

5
00:00:35.248 --> 00:00:42.922
Through E draw E F parallel to A D, and through D draw F D parallel to C E by One Thirty-One.

6
00:00:42.922 --> 00:00:53.320
Since angles F E B and E F D are less than two right angles, produce E B and F D by Postulate Five to meet at G, and join A G.

7
00:00:53.740 --> 00:01:03.703
In the isosceles right triangles A C E and E C B, each base angle is half a right angle by One Five and One Thirty-Two, so angle A E B is right.

8
00:01:03.703 --> 00:01:16.226
By One Fifteen, One Twenty-Nine, One Thirty-Four, and One Six, triangles B D G and E F G are also isosceles right triangles, so G D equals B D, and G F equals E F, which equals C D.

9
00:01:16.226 --> 00:01:31.863
By the Pythagorean Theorem, One Forty-Seven, since C E equals A C, the square on E A is double the square on A C; and since F G equals E F, the square on E G is double the square on E F, and therefore double the square on C D.

10
00:01:31.863 --> 00:01:42.449
Applying One Forty-Seven to right triangles A E G and A D G, the squares on A D and D G equal the square on A G, which equals the squares on E A and E G.

11
00:01:42.449 --> 00:01:48.469
Since D G equals D B, the squares on A D and D B are double the squares on A C and C D.

12
00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:50.060
Which was to be proved.

13
00:01:50.480 --> 00:02:00.297
Proposition Two Ten proves the algebraic identity: the quantity two a plus b squared, plus b squared, equals twice the sum of a squared and a plus b squared.

14
00:02:00.297 --> 00:02:19.181
As Proclus and Theon of Smyrna record, the Pythagoreans used this exact theorem to generate successive side and diagonal numbers—one and one, two and three, five and seven, twelve and seventeen—satisfying two x squared minus y squared equals plus or minus one, and converging to the square root of two.

15
00:02:19.181 --> 00:02:32.000
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G ninety-two synthesizes nineteen direct prerequisites across sixty-three ancestor nodes, uniting the parallel postulate, isosceles angle chasing, and the Pythagorean theorem.

16
